 for if every tool could perform its own work when
 ordered, or by seeing what to do in advance, like
the statues of Daedalus in the story, or the tripods
of Hephaestus which the poet says 'enter self-moved
  the company divine,' — if thus shuttles wove and
quills played harps of themselves, master-craftsmen
would have no need of assistants and masters no need
                     of slaves.
            - Aristotle, Politics 1253b
